  "name": "Tingkilan (Kutai Malay pantun song)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Malay song of the Kutai of Tenggarong, East Kalimantan, whose name refers to taunting or answering through pantun; it developed with the coming of Islam to Kutai and is heard at the Erau festival, weddings and other customary occasions. Its original ensemble was a gambus lute (the oud card stands in) and two small ketipung drums (the generic membrane-drum card stands in), later joined by kendang and violin, while singers trade pantun verses in Kutai Malay with each other and with the audience.",
  "recipe": "classic modern tingkilan, functional Song. duet. oud, membrane drum pulse resonant acoustic membrane drum, melodic bowed song accompaniment fiddle. field recording. ORTF stereo pair, portable field-recorder pre. 24-bit digital.",
